// cSpell: ignore fifi
// Skia font rendering is not consistent across platforms
//ignore:skia

// Same `fi` ligature cut as `text-selection-ligature`, but with the input on a fractional device
// pixel. The boundary is snapped to a whole pixel once, where it is computed, and the highlight
// rectangle and the glyph clip are both built from that one value -- yet they still have to agree
// after the renderer adds the item's own offset. Quantizing the two differently there (rounding the
// clip, truncating the rectangle) left a one pixel column of unselected glyph sitting on top of the
// highlight, which is the artifact the clipping approach exists to remove.
//
// Qt and Skia pixel-align the origin before drawing a text input, and femtovg never quantizes
// either edge, so only the software renderer can show this.
TestCase := Window {
    width: 64px;
    height: 40px;

    // The `i` half is selected, so the highlight's leading edge is the cut through the ligature:
    // the `f` half must stay black right up to it, without a black column on the highlight.
    TextInput {
        x: 0.5px;
        y: 0;
        width: 60px;
        height: 40px;
        text: "fi";
        font-size: 28px;
        color: black;
        selection-background-color: #c8c8c8;
        selection-foreground-color: red;
        anchor-position-byte-offset: 1;
        cursor-position-byte-offset: 2;
    }
}
